Basic Crontab Management Commands

To configure user-level cron tasks, use the crontab utility. Avoid editing the /var/spool/ text files directly to prevent syntax corruption.

1) to create new job or edit the existing job use crontab command
Edit your crontab: Opens the table in a text editor (like nano or vim). Saves are automatically checked for basic syntax errors before application.

crontab -e

2)List your active cron jobs: Outputs your current schedule to the terminal window

crontab -l

3)Remove your crontab entirely: Deletes all cron entries(jobs) of user who executed this command

crontab -r

4)Manage another user's crontab (requires root privileges):

sudo crontab -u username -e

